{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"f8e541a61b64629c","type":"group","x":-2000,"y":-1140,"width":4000,"height":2280,"label":"Quantum Algorithms for Quantum Chemistry and Quantum Materials Science"},
		{"id":"dfb53a6033730a20","type":"group","x":-1420,"y":100,"width":3355,"height":580,"label":"Algorithm Architectures & Simulation Primitives"},
		{"id":"86e7607e145f6a76","type":"group","x":-1420,"y":-460,"width":2725,"height":500,"label":"Simulation Challenges in Molecular & Materials Science"},
		{"id":"dde33e1ac46baf2b","type":"group","x":-1420,"y":-980,"width":2085,"height":460,"label":"Introduction & Computational Foundations"},
		{"id":"c84824ee29fc2fd3","type":"group","x":-1420,"y":740,"width":1067,"height":320,"label":"Evaluation and Readout"},
		{
			"id":"d649106170311733",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Quantum Algorithms for Quantum Chemistry and Quantum Materials Science",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1980,
			"y":-1120,
			"width":555,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"f9d936d4390f275e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Simulation Challenges in Molecular & Materials Science",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1405,
			"y":-440,
			"width":418,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"210e5bb7b494ce67",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Introduction & Computational Foundations",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1405,
			"y":-960,
			"width":385,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"07152ef28967b8ae",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Algorithm Architectures & Simulation Primitives",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1395,
			"y":120,
			"width":418,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"71e66d3f0c76138f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## [[Quantum Chemistry]]\nFocuses on solving low-lying electronic eigenstates under the fixed-nucleus [[Born-Oppenheimer Approximation]].\n\nDistinguishes between easily handled _single-reference_ problems (dominant configuration) and strongly correlated _multireference_ setups (multiple configuration interaction).\n\nPrimary targets include metalloenzyme active sites (e.g., nitrogenase, oxygen-evolving complex), transition metal nanocatalysts, and light-harvesting pigments.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1360,
			"y":-300,
			"width":580,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"262ef212d611dc1e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## [[Variational Quantum Eigensolvers]]\nImplements a hybrid quantum-classical optimization feedback loop to iteratively lower trial state energies on near-term hardware.\n\n[[Ansatz Typologies]]: [[Unitary Coupled Cluster]] (UCC), Hardware-Efficient structures, dynamic Adapt-VQE pooling, and Tensor Networks (MPS, MERA, deep MERA).\n\nPrimary Bottlenecks: Sampling noise demands high repetition overhead (errors scale as $1/\\epsilon^2$), alongside classical parameter optimization traps (local minima).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-245,
			"y":260,
			"width":545,
			"height":300
		},
		{
			"id":"3a0cf3ae840ac290",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Prescribed-Path State Preparation\n _[[Adiabatic State Preparation]]_: Evolves a system slowly along a continuous path; runtime is constrained by the minimum spectral gap ($g_{min}$).\n    \n_[[Quantum Imaginary-Time Evolution]] (QITE)_: Avoids optimization loops by projecting ground states using unitaries calculated from localized geometric qubit domains.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-760,
			"y":260,
			"width":500,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"d2935450a00eea55",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Qubit Representation & Encodings\n[[First Quantization]]: Uses Configuration Interaction (CI) spaces to map electrons to qubit registers, scaling logarithmically with basis functions in the continuum limit.\n\n[[Second Quantization]]: Tracks orbital occupation numbers across Fock space, natively enforcing Fermi statistics at the operator level.\n\nElectronic Basis Selection: Balances compact wave function representation (Gaussian atomic bases) against structural sparsity and periodic simplicity (Plane waves with pseudopotentials).\n\nFermion-to-Qubit Mappings: Transitions operators to Pauli matrices via frameworks like **[[Jordan-Wigner]]** (costly non-local strings) or **[[Bravyi-Kitaev]]** (optimizes operation weight to $O(\\log N)$).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1350,
			"y":260,
			"width":570,
			"height":400
		},
		{
			"id":"5980201e3b501c0b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Hybrid Systems & Extensions\nExcited state formulations via [[Quantum Subspace Expansion]] (QSE) or [[Equation-of-Motion]] (EOM) generalized eigenvalue techniques.\n\n[[Finite-temperature algorithms]] designed to compute complex thermal averages.\n\n**[[Quantum Embedding]]**: Multiscale partitioning coupling a highly correlated quantum register subsystem to a classically simulated environment (e.g., DMFT).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1400,
			"y":260,
			"width":520,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"b7dd7f7107e2aa16",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## [[Correlated Electronic Structure in Materials]]\nStudies strongly interacting systems where effective non-interacting band theories fail, such as **[[Mott insulators]]**.\n\nInvestigates macro-scale physics including high-temperature superconductivity phase diagrams, non-Fermi liquids (strange metals), flat-band Moiré materials (twisted bilayer graphene), and frustrated topological spin liquids.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":320,
			"y":-300,
			"width":480,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"4b482898d8f2b3b9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## [[Quantum Phase Estimation]]\nUses controlled time-evolution blocks ($e^{-it\\hat{H}}$) to project trial wave functions directly onto exact energy eigenstates.\n\nIterative QPE variations trade multi-qubit measurement registers for intensive classical post-processing.\n\nAchieves precise results scaling as $1/\\epsilon$, making it a core target for future error-corrected, fault-tolerant platforms.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":320,
			"y":260,
			"width":500,
			"height":260
		},
		{
			"id":"4bcecdd131a7fde6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Benchmark Systems\n\nUtilizing classical verification standards (e.g., density functional theory, coupled cluster limits, quantum Monte Carlo) to establish a rigorous baseline for true quantum advantage.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1361,
			"y":880,
			"width":581,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"e29fca13e3cfbe1e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Data Readout Methods\nExtracting targeted physical insights through equal-time expectation measurements or time-dependent Green's functions",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-760,
			"y":880,
			"width":381,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"8dabab1d5fd32739",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Quantum Information Fundamentals",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1360,
			"y":-820,
			"width":295,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"e5675ab266754e4c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Hamiltonian Simulation\nProduct Formulas: Standard [[Trotter-Suzuki]] mappings step through individual interacting terms, alongside optimized multiproduct formulas that limit circuit depth.\n    \nAdvanced Primitives: Linear Combination of Unitaries (LCU) Taylor expansions, Quantum Walks, and Quantum Signal Processing (QSP) / Qubitization block encodings.\n    \nLocality Exploitation: Divide-and-conquer methodologies leverage Lieb-Robinson bounds to simulate lattice setups linearly with space-time volume.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":840,
			"y":260,
			"width":500,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"33e396d22c305a9b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## [[Dynamical Quantum Effects in Materials]]\nModels out-of-equilibrium realities including mesoscopic electron transport, neutron scattering metrics, and ultrafast pump-probe laser responses.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":820,
			"y":-300,
			"width":460,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"14ebccf0a962b106",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Evaluation and Readout",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1406,
			"y":760,
			"width":210,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"83baf72d95fcfe86",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## [[Quantum Molecular Spectroscopy]]\nAims to compute the highly anharmonic eigenstates of the nuclear Schrödinger equation to interpret rovibrational transitions.\n\nExemplified by highly fluxional \"floppy\" molecules like $\\text{CH}_5^+$ or the moving networks of hydrogen-bonded water clusters.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-760,
			"y":-300,
			"width":500,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"46c7d95316ef9273",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Quantum Complexity Frameworks\n\nReal-time quantum dynamics simulates physical systems efficiently and falls under the BQP (bounded-error quantum polynomial) complexity class.\n\nGround-state determination and finite-temperature thermal averages belong to the harder **QMA** (quantum Merlin-Arthur) complexity class, meaning they lack general polynomial-time guarantees on quantum computers.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-972,
			"y":-820,
			"width":532,
			"height":280
		},
		{
			"id":"479aac1d41c73306",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Quantum Computing Paradigm Models\n\nAnalogue Quantum Simulation: Utilizing a controllable physical system (such as cold atomic gases in optical lattices) to directly mimic target quantum behavior.\n\nDigital Quantum Simulation: A circuit-based approach mapping instructions into a sequence of discrete, few-qubit quantum gates.\n    \nAdiabatic Quantum Computation: Moving slowly along a time-dependent Hamiltonian path to continuously track the ground state.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-420,
			"y":-820,
			"width":560,
			"height":280
		},
		{
			"id":"4b2142713695cf5f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Hardware and Error Realities\n**NISQ (Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum) Era**: Current small-scale devices susceptible to qubit decoherence and gate errors.\n\n**Quantum Error Correction (QEC)**: Mitigation of faults by encoding single, error-corrected **logical qubits** into highly entangled arrays of physical qubits (e.g., surface codes), incurring massive hardware overhead.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":160,
			"y":-820,
			"width":480,
			"height":280
		},
		{"id":"3ced18883593c2a0","type":"text","text":"## [[Chemical Quantum Dynamics]]\nTracks non-equilibrium electronic and nuclear motion subjected to external fields.\n\nAddresses the breakdown of the [[Born-Oppenheimer Approximation]] at **conical intersections** (nonadiabatic quantum effects).\n\nTargets complex mechanisms such as [[Proton-Coupled Electron Transfer]] (PCET), complex condensed-phase environments (Kubo theory limitations), and hot plasmonic chemistry.","x":-240,"y":-300,"width":540,"height":320}
	],
